{
  "abstract": "We construct directed strongly regular graphs with parameters $(65,14,4,3,3)$ and $(77,13,4,3,2)$, resolving two open complementary pairs in the Hobart–Brouwer table. Their complements have parameters $(65,50,40,38,40)$ and $(77,63,54,51,54)$. Both constructions use the closed adjacency matrix $B=A+I$ and mixed column classes. A five-by-three table over $\\mathbb Z_{13}$ gives $B^2=2B+3J$ at order 65; an eleven-by-five table over $\\mathbb Z_7$ gives $B^2=3B+2J$ at order 77. Direct graph and complement checks and independent cyclic coefficient identities verify the literal matrices exactly. An elementary shared-quotient lifting principle then permits local row decompositions to be chosen independently. We exhaust these decompositions for both displayed quotients. At order 65 there are six choices at each of twenty-six pair classes, yielding $6^{26}$ split systems and $(6^{26}+432)/13$ isomorphism classes in each of two disjoint orientations. Exactly thirty-six classes per orientation have automorphism group $C_{13}$; all others are asymmetric. At order 77, seven binary choices give twenty classes per orientation, two with automorphism group $C_7$ and eighteen asymmetric. Quotient automorphism proofs establish the classifications. Separate nauty computations check the entire smaller family and a deterministic sample of the larger one. Standalone standard-library code constructs both base graphs, exhausts the local domains and verifies the classification arguments without a solver or the discovery search. Explicit good partitions also place both new seeds in infinite extensions using Gyürki's published theorem. The results classify the two displayed quotients only; neither a classification of all graphs with these parameters nor a new general lifting operation is claimed.",
